        <!-- ============ CHANGELOG ============ -->
        <section id="changelog" class="well-band py-20 sm:py-32 scroll-mt-16" aria-labelledby="changelog-heading">
            <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
                <h2 id="changelog-heading" class="t-section mb-6">Changelog</h2>
                <p class="t-sub mb-12">What’s new, straight from the workshop. The version number is the date, no decoder ring needed.</p>

                <div>
                    <article class="log-entry">
                        <span class="log-version">2026.7.30</span>
                        <span class="log-date">&ensp;July 30, 2026</span>
                        <h3>The big one</h3>
                        <p>
                            A formatting toolbar with ⌘B, ⌘I, and ⌘K. Find &amp; replace (⌘F). An outline panel.
                            Automatic version history. Templates. Smart paste that turns web clippings into clean markdown.
                            Typewriter scrolling, word goals with a progress ring, themed HTML export, and one-click .zip
                            backup of every document. Math with KaTeX, diagrams with Mermaid, send-to for ChatGPT, Claude,
                            and Perplexity, a built-in markdown guide (⌘/), a redesigned Documents view, and the whole app
                            hand-translated into 70 languages.
                        </p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="log-entry">
                        <span class="log-version">2025.1.26</span>
                        <span class="log-date">&ensp;January 26, 2025</span>
                        <h3>The fun one</h3>
                        <p>
                            The era of themes and toys: eight color themes and the chaos zone grew up here, the pet cat,
                            Matrix rain, vaporwave mode, the combo counter, typewriter sounds, and friends, alongside a
                            long tail of preview and performance polish.
                        </p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="log-entry">
                        <span class="log-version">1.2</span>
                        <span class="log-date">&ensp;October 26, 2024</span>
                        <h3>The beginning</h3>
                        <p>
                            A fast, private markdown editor with live GitHub-flavored preview, offline documents that never
                            leave your device, and one-click HTML export. Small, quick, and quietly determined to stay that way.
                        </p>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
        <section id="questions" class="py-20 sm:py-32 scroll-mt-16" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
            <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
                <h2 id="faq-heading" class="t-section mb-10">Questions &amp;&nbsp;answers</h2>

                <div>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>Is Markdown Editor free?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">The browser extension is completely free on Chrome, Edge, and Firefox, every feature included. On Apple devices, the Mac, iPad, and iPhone app (which includes the Safari extension) is a single $5 purchase. No subscription, ever.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>Do all platforms really get the same features?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">Yes, it’s a house rule. Every feature ships on every platform: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, Mac, iPad, and iPhone. If something can’t work everywhere, we don’t ship it anywhere.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>Does it work offline?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">Completely. Everything runs locally, you can write a novel on a plane with airplane mode on. Nothing about the editor needs the internet.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>Where are my documents stored?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">On your own device, in the browser’s or app’s local storage. Nothing is uploaded and there’s no account. You can download any document as a .md file, or back up your entire library as a .zip, whenever you want.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>What flavor of markdown is supported?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">GitHub-flavored markdown, tables, task lists, strikethrough, plus footnotes, front matter, highlights, KaTeX math equations, and Mermaid diagrams.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>What languages is it available in?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">Seventy, every language the Chrome Web Store supports, from Arabic to Zulu, each one hand-translated rather than machine-dumped.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="faq">
                        <summary>OK, but what is the chaos zone?
                            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="faq-body">A drawer of completely optional toys hiding in Settings: a pet cat that wanders your screen, Matrix rain, vaporwave mode, a fighting-game combo counter, typewriter sounds, emoji rain, a rage-quit alarm, and more. Everything is off by default. We just believe software is allowed to be fun.</div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

